    {
      "path": ".claude/scripts/with-browser-lock.sh",
      "content": "A discovery failure is indistinguishable from a successful scan with no remaining members. If ps cannot be launched, exits unsuccessfully, or produces output that the parser rejects, signal_all() returns zero; after the command is reaped, the cleanup loop then releases the lock even if detached descendants are still running. A partial scan also overwrites %known and can permanently lose orphaned members. Check the ps exit status and validate the snapshot before replacing %known; propagate discovery failures separately and retry without treating them as proof that cleanup is complete. Add failure-injection coverage for this path.",
      "existing_code": "  open(my $ps, \"-|\", \"/bin/ps\", \"-axwwE\", \"-o\", \"pid=,ppid=,pgid=,stat=,lstart=,command=\") or return {};",
      "start_line": 69,
      "end_line": 69,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "path": ".claude/scripts/with-browser-lock.sh",
      "content": "lstart has only one-second resolution, so it is not a unique process identity. If a remembered descendant exits and its PID is reused by an unrelated process started within the same second, this comparison admits that process and signal_all() sends it TERM/KILL, violating B14. The new stale-PID test only plants the string \"stale\", so it cannot detect this collision. Use a higher-resolution kernel start identity and test matching displayed timestamps with different actual identities. Separately, a fresh ps scan alone does not eliminate the scan-to-kill PID-reuse race, so an absolute no-reuse guarantee requires an identity-safe signalling mechanism.",
      "existing_code": "      || (($known{$x} // \"\") eq $p{$x}{start});",
      "start_line": 84,
      "end_line": 84,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    }
  ],
